>>> [ 17] 3.2 
    [ 18] Pantograph Control 
>>> [ 19] 3.2.1 
    [ 20] Pantograph -  Lifting 
>>> [ 21] 3.2.1.1 
    [ 22] Function Design 
>>> [ 23] 3.2.1.1.1 Normal Condition 
    [ 24] Lifting the pantograph is a precondition for collecting energy from catenary system. 
    [ 25] Lifting can be performed automatically or manually. In any case, the driver selects a line voltage system (4-position selector 
    [ 26] switch) and switches "raise pantograph 1" or "raise pantograph 2".  
    [ 27] Country Code is needed to identify pantograph which is needed depending on country of operation. 
    [ 28] The identified pantograph are lifted up on request. 
    [ 29] TCMS sends to PCU by IP bus which type of pantograph has to be lifted. TCMS also commands 2 different DOs in order to 
    [ 30] define which type of pantograph has to be lifted. 
>>> [ 31] 3.2.1.1.2 Failure Condition 
    [ 32] 1) Pantograph doesn't lift up although an order was given. 
    [ 33] 2) Two pantographs of the selected type are lifting up. (unless for ice scraping) 
    [ 34] 3) Lifting of pantograph without order. 
    [ 35] 4) Wrong type of pantograph is lifting up. 
    [ 36] 5) Pantograph switch is in "up" position although related pantograph is lowered. 
>>> [ 37] 3.2.1.1.3 Failure Supervision 
    [ 38] 1) If the pantograph is not raised after 35 seconds after the raise order and the main reservoir pressure is higher than 750 kPa, 
    [ 39] the pantograph is considered faulty. 
    [ 40] If no air pressure is detected at the pantograph 5 minutes after the raise order and the main reservoir pressure is less than or 
    [ 41] equal to 750 kPa, the pantograph is considered faulty. 
    [ 42] 2) Both pantographs will be ordered down. 
    [ 43] 3) Pantograph will be lowered, considered faulty and blocked additionally an event will be set. 
    [ 44] 4) system detection detects invalid line voltage system or country code does not fit to raised pantograph. In both cases all 
    [ 45] pantographs will be lowered and a failure message will be displayed. 
    [ 46] 5) TCMS ignores pantograph-switch and pantograph remains in lowered position. IDU indicates that switch has to be turned to 
    [ 47] "down" position. 
>>> [ 48] 3.2.1.2 
    [ 49] Requirements 
    [ 50]  
    [ 51] ID 
>>> [ 52] 2F_04.01.Zefiro-Europe.TRS.619 
    [ 53] Description 
    [ 54] The following sequence shall be implemented: 
    [ 55] ∗ 
    [ 56] Driver selects the Country where the train is operated from the IDU. 
    [ 57] ∗ 
    [ 58] Driver selects the line voltage system 
    [ 59] ∗ 
    [ 60] The (not blocked or cut out) pantograph (one per 8 car train unit) for the chosen line voltage system shall be lifted 
    [ 61] when requested by the driver 
    [ 62] ∗ 
    [ 63] Line voltage shall be detected and the chosen line voltage system verified 
    [ 64] If the detection and verification are successful, the closing of line circuit breakers shall be enabled. ELSE a diagnostic 
    [ 65] event shall be generated. 
    [ 66] Safety level 
    [ 67] N/A 
    [ 68]  

